// Sorting: putting a sequence in order, in place, with no storage but its own.
//
// `Sort` is an introsort -- quicksort with a three-way partition, falling back to heapsort once the recursion budget
// is spent and to insertion sort on small partitions. Each part covers the others' weakness. Quicksort is the
// fastest of the three on ordinary input and the only one that degrades to quadratic on input chosen to defeat its
// pivot rule; heapsort never degrades and is slower on everything; insertion sort beats both below about sixteen
// elements, where choosing a pivot costs more than the sorting does. The result has a genuine O(n log n) worst case
// with no auxiliary storage, which no one of the three has alone.
//
// **The worst case, precisely.** Comparisons are O(n log n) for every input, with no exceptions and nothing
// probabilistic about it: the depth budget is `2 * floor(log2 n)`, and a range still unsorted when it runs out is
// handed to heapsort, which is O(n log n) unconditionally. Auxiliary storage is O(1) -- one pivot and a few indices.
// Stack depth is O(log n) frames, about sixty-four for any addressable length, because the recursive call always
// takes the smaller side of a partition while the larger is handled by the loop. None of that depends on the input
// being random, and there are no randomized pivots to make it depend on the run.
//
// **Sorting is not stable.** Equivalent elements may come out in a different order than they went in. It is
// deterministic -- the same input always gives the same output and the same number of comparisons -- but a caller
// who needs equal elements to keep their original order needs a stable sort, which needs somewhere to put things.
//
// The plain forms require `<` on the element type and nothing else, under the same strict-weak-ordering contract as
// `Search`: equivalence is derived as neither element being less than the other, so a type that orders without
// defining `==` sorts fine. The `By` forms take a three-way comparison instead, for a sequence ordered by something
// other than its element type's own `<`.
//
// The two are separate names rather than overloads of one. Rux resolves an untyped integer literal against a single
// candidate but not against an overload set, so adding an overload would have made every existing call stop
// compiling. See Docs/CompilerDefects.md.
//
// The `By` machinery below mirrors the operator-based helpers rather than sharing them. A generic function cannot be
// used as a value in Rux, so the operator forms cannot pass `<` as a comparator into one shared implementation, and
// threading a runtime flag instead would put a branch in the innermost comparison of every sort -- the one place it
// would be felt. When generic functions become usable as values, the two sets collapse into one.

// Below this many elements, insertion sort beats partitioning: the cost of
// choosing a pivot dominates, and partitions left by quicksort are already
// nearly ordered, which is insertion sort's best case.
const InsertionSortThreshold: uint = 16;

import Core::{ MutableSlice, Ordering };

/// Puts `items` into non-decreasing order.
///
/// `items` is borrowed mutably and rewritten. Fewer than two elements are already sorted, so the data pointer is
/// never read in that case and an empty view is not a special case.
///
/// Requires `<` on the element type, as a strict weak ordering. Equal elements may be reordered: this is not stable.
///
/// Cannot fail. Makes O(n log n) comparisons for every input, uses O(1) storage beyond the sequence, and recurses
/// O(log n) deep.
///
/// https://rux-lang.dev/docs/api/algorithms/sort
pub func Sort<T>(items: MutableSlice<T>) {
    let data = items.data;
    let length = items.length;
    if length < 2 { return; }

    // 2*floor(log2 n) is Musser's bound: quicksort's expected recursion depth
    // is about 1.39*log2 n, so the budget is only exhausted by input that is
    // actively defeating the pivot rule. The floor of one keeps the budget
    // meaningful for the smallest ranges that reach here.
    var budget = FloorLog2(length) * 2;
    if budget == 0 {
        budget = 1;
    }
    IntroSortRange<T>(data, 0, length, budget);
}

/// Puts `items` into non-increasing order.
///
/// `items` is borrowed mutably and rewritten. An ascending sort followed by a reversal, rather than a mirrored copy
/// of every helper below: threading a direction flag through the partition and both fallbacks would cost a branch in
/// the innermost comparison, and duplicating the helpers would double the amount of subtle index arithmetic to
/// maintain, all for one extra linear pass. Sorting is unstable in either direction, so reversing costs nothing in
/// ordering either.
///
/// Requires `<` on the element type. Equal elements may be reordered.
///
/// Cannot fail. Makes O(n log n) comparisons and uses O(1) storage beyond the sequence.
///
/// https://rux-lang.dev/docs/api/algorithms/sort-descending
pub func SortDescending<T>(items: MutableSlice<T>) {
    Sort<T>(items);
    Reverse<T>(items);
}

/// Puts the element that belongs at `n` in a fully sorted `items` at `n`, and partitions around it.
///
/// `items` is borrowed mutably and rewritten; `n` is read by value. Afterwards everything before `n` is at most the
/// element at `n` and everything after is at least it. Neither side is ordered any further, which is the whole
/// point: this is what to reach for when only one order statistic is wanted -- a median, a percentile -- because it
/// is linear on average where a full sort is not.
///
/// Reports false, changing nothing, when `n` is not an index into `items`. That is the only way this fails.
///
/// Quickselect over the same partition `Sort` uses, but iterative: only one side of a partition can contain `n`, so
/// there is nothing to recurse into and the stack stays flat. The same depth budget bounds the worst case at
/// O(n log n), against O(n) on average.
///
/// Requires `<` on the element type. Equal elements may be reordered.
///
/// https://rux-lang.dev/docs/api/algorithms/nth-element
pub func NthElement<T>(items: MutableSlice<T>, n: uint) -> bool {
    let data = items.data;
    let length = items.length;
    if n >= length { return false; }

    var lo: uint = 0;
    var hi = length;
    var budget = FloorLog2(length) * 2;
    if budget == 0 {
        budget = 1;
    }

    while hi - lo > InsertionSortThreshold {
        if budget == 0 {
            // Ordering the rest satisfies the postcondition too, and bounds
            // the worst case at O(n log n).
            HeapSortRange<T>(data, lo, hi);
            return true;
        }
        budget--;

        let middle = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
        let pivotIndex = MedianOfThree<T>(data, lo, middle, hi - 1);
        let pivot = data[pivotIndex];

        var lessEnd: uint = 0;
        var greaterStart: uint = 0;
        PartitionThreeWay<T>(data, lo, hi, pivot, @lessEnd, @greaterStart);

        if n < lessEnd {
            hi = lessEnd;
        }
        else if n < greaterStart {
            // n fell inside the band equivalent to the pivot, so the element
            // there is already the one that belongs at n.
            return true;
        }
        else {
            lo = greaterStart;
        }
    }

    InsertionSortRange<T>(data, lo, hi);
    return true;
}

/// Puts the `count` smallest elements of `items` in order at its front.
///
/// `items` is borrowed mutably and rewritten; `count` is read by value. The first `count` positions hold the
/// smallest elements in non-decreasing order, and the rest hold everything else in no particular order.
///
/// Reports false, changing nothing, when `count` is larger than the length. A `count` of zero succeeds and does
/// nothing.
///
/// A max-heap over the first `count` elements, which each later element is compared against and displaces only if it
/// is smaller: O(n log count) comparisons, against the O(n log n) a full sort would cost. That is worth having when
/// `count` is much smaller than the length and not otherwise.
///
/// Requires `<` on the element type. Equal elements may be reordered.
///
/// https://rux-lang.dev/docs/api/algorithms/partial-sort
pub func PartialSort<T>(items: MutableSlice<T>, count: uint) -> bool {
    let data = items.data;
    let length = items.length;
    if count > length { return false; }
    if count == 0 { return true; }

    // A max-heap over the first count elements: its root is the largest of the
    // candidates, which is exactly the one a smaller element should displace.
    var i = count / 2;
    while i > 0 {
        i--;
        SiftDown<T>(data, 0, i, count);
    }

    var j = count;
    while j < length {
        if data[j] < data[0] {
            SwapAt<T>(data, 0, j);
            SiftDown<T>(data, 0, 0, count);
        }
        j++;
    }

    // The candidates are now the count smallest, but in heap order rather than
    // sorted order.
    HeapSortRange<T>(data, 0, count);
    return true;
}

// Internal helper; not part of the package API.
//
// floor(log2(value)), and zero for both 0 and 1. A shift loop rather than a
// call into Rux/Math, whose logarithms are floating-point only and would drag
// a dependency and a rounding question into an integer budget.
func FloorLog2(value: uint) -> uint {
    var result: uint = 0;
    var remaining = value;
    while remaining > 1 {
        remaining = remaining >> 1;
        result++;
    }
    return result;
}

// Internal helper; not part of the package API.
func SwapAt<T>(data: *var T, first: uint, second: uint) {
    if first == second { return; }

    let temp = data[first];
    data[first] = data[second];
    data[second] = temp;
}

// Internal helper; not part of the package API.
//
// The index of the median of three elements, using only `<`. Sampling three
// positions rather than taking the first element is what keeps already-sorted
// and reverse-sorted input off the quadratic path.
func MedianOfThree<T>(data: *T, first: uint, middle: uint, last: uint) -> uint {
    if data[first] < data[middle] {
        if data[middle] < data[last] { return middle; }
        if data[first] < data[last] { return last; }
        return first;
    }

    if data[first] < data[last] { return first; }
    if data[middle] < data[last] { return last; }
    return middle;
}

// Internal helper; not part of the package API.
//
// Rearranges [low, high) into elements less than the pivot, then elements
// equivalent to it, then elements greater, writing the two boundaries out.
//
// Three-way rather than two-way because the equivalent band is then excluded
// from both recursions: an all-equal sequence becomes a single linear pass
// instead of the quadratic behavior a two-way partition produces on it, and
// input drawn from a small set of distinct values stops being a worst case.
func PartitionThreeWay<T>(data: *var T, low: uint, high: uint, pivot: T, lessEnd: *var uint,
                          greaterStart: *var uint) {
    var less = low;
    var index = low;
    var greater = high;

    while index < greater {
        if data[index] < pivot {
            SwapAt<T>(data, index, less);
            less++;
            index++;
        }
        else {
            if pivot < data[index] {
                // greater is strictly above index here because the loop
                // condition holds, so this cannot wrap.
                greater--;
                SwapAt<T>(data, index, greater);
            }
            else {
                index++;
            }
        }
    }

    *lessEnd = less;
    *greaterStart = greater;
}

// Internal helper; not part of the package API.
/// Provides the public `InsertionSortRange` operation.
///
/// https://rux-lang.dev/docs/api/algorithms/insertion-sort-range
pub func InsertionSortRange<T>(data: *var T, low: uint, high: uint) {
    if high - low < 2 { return; }

    var i = low + 1;
    while i < high {
        let key = data[i];
        var j = i;
        // `j > low` is the loop condition rather than half of a compound
        // guard, so data[j - 1] is never formed at j == low, where the
        // decrement would wrap.
        while j > low {
            if !(key < data[j - 1]) { break; }
            data[j] = data[j - 1];
            j--;
        }
        data[j] = key;
        i++;
    }
}

// Internal helper; not part of the package API.
//
// Restores the max-heap property at start within the first count elements of
// the heap based at low. Iterative, so heapsort adds nothing to the stack.
func SiftDown<T>(data: *var T, low: uint, start: uint, count: uint) {
    var root = start;
    loop {
        let child = root * 2 + 1;
        if child >= count { return; }

        var largest = child;
        let right = child + 1;
        if right < count {
            if data[low + child] < data[low + right] {
                largest = right;
            }
        }

        if !(data[low + root] < data[low + largest]) { return; }
        SwapAt<T>(data, low + root, low + largest);
        root = largest;
    }
}

// Internal helper; not part of the package API.
//
// The fallback that bounds the worst case. O(n log n) whatever the input, and
// O(1) space, at a larger constant factor than quicksort — which is why it is
// reached only once the recursion budget is spent.
/// Provides the public `HeapSortRange` operation.
///
/// https://rux-lang.dev/docs/api/algorithms/heap-sort-range
pub func HeapSortRange<T>(data: *var T, low: uint, high: uint) {
    let count = high - low;
    if count < 2 { return; }

    var i = count / 2;
    while i > 0 {
        i--;
        SiftDown<T>(data, low, i, count);
    }

    var end = count;
    while end > 1 {
        end--;
        SwapAt<T>(data, low, low + end);
        SiftDown<T>(data, low, 0, end);
    }
}

// Internal helper; not part of the package API.
func IntroSortRange<T>(data: *var T, low: uint, high: uint, depth: uint) {
    var lo = low;
    var hi = high;
    var budget = depth;

    while hi - lo > InsertionSortThreshold {
        if budget == 0 {
            HeapSortRange<T>(data, lo, hi);
            return;
        }
        budget--;

        let middle = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
        let pivotIndex = MedianOfThree<T>(data, lo, middle, hi - 1);
        let pivot = data[pivotIndex];

        var lessEnd: uint = 0;
        var greaterStart: uint = 0;
        PartitionThreeWay<T>(data, lo, hi, pivot, @lessEnd, @greaterStart);

        // Recurse into the smaller side and loop on the larger. The recursive
        // call then covers at most half the range, which bounds the stack at
        // about log2(n) frames — 64 or so for any addressable length —
        // regardless of how badly the pivots are chosen.
        if lessEnd - lo < hi - greaterStart {
            IntroSortRange<T>(data, lo, lessEnd, budget);
            lo = greaterStart;
        }
        else {
            IntroSortRange<T>(data, greaterStart, hi, budget);
            hi = lessEnd;
        }
    }

    InsertionSortRange<T>(data, lo, hi);
}

// Each sort above also takes an explicit ordering, for a sequence ordered by
// something other than its element type's own `<`. The comparator answers
// "does a come strictly before b" and must be a strict weak ordering, exactly
// as `<` must be; equivalence is derived from it alone, so a comparator is
// enough on its own and the element type need not define any operator.
//
// The machinery below mirrors the operator-based helpers rather than sharing
// them. A generic function cannot be used as a value in Rux, so the operator
// forms cannot simply pass `<` as a comparator to one shared implementation.
// Threading a runtime flag instead would put a branch in the innermost
// comparison of every sort, which is the one place it would be felt. When
// generic functions become usable as values, the two sets collapse into one.

/// Puts `items` into the order `compare` describes, smallest first.
///
/// `items` is borrowed mutably and rewritten; `compare` is called O(n log n) times and must be a strict weak
/// ordering, exactly as `<` must be. Equivalence is derived from it alone, so the element type need not define any
/// operator at all.
///
/// Cannot fail. Makes O(n log n) comparisons for every input, uses O(1) storage beyond the sequence, and recurses
/// O(log n) deep. Equal elements may be reordered: this is not stable.
///
/// https://rux-lang.dev/docs/api/algorithms/sort-by
pub func SortBy<T>(items: MutableSlice<T>, compare: func(T, T) -> Ordering) {
    let data = items.data;
    let length = items.length;
    if length < 2 { return; }

    var budget = FloorLog2(length) * 2;
    if budget == 0 {
        budget = 1;
    }
    IntroSortRangeBy<T>(data, 0, length, budget, compare);
}

/// Puts `items` into the reverse of the order `compare` describes.
///
/// `items` is borrowed mutably and rewritten; `compare` is called O(n log n) times. An ascending sort followed by a
/// reversal, for the same reason `SortDescending` is.
///
/// Cannot fail. Makes O(n log n) comparisons and uses O(1) storage beyond the sequence. Equal elements may be
/// reordered.
///
/// https://rux-lang.dev/docs/api/algorithms/sort-descending-by
pub func SortDescendingBy<T>(items: MutableSlice<T>, compare: func(T, T) -> Ordering) {
    SortBy<T>(items, compare);
    Reverse<T>(items);
}

/// Puts the element that belongs at `n` under `compare` at `n`, and partitions around it.
///
/// `items` is borrowed mutably and rewritten; `n` is read by value; `compare` must be a strict weak ordering.
/// Exactly `NthElement`, with the order supplied rather than taken from the element type.
///
/// Reports false, changing nothing, when `n` is not an index into `items`.
///
/// O(n) comparisons on average and O(n log n) at worst. Equivalent elements may be reordered.
///
/// https://rux-lang.dev/docs/api/algorithms/nth-element-by
pub func NthElementBy<T>(items: MutableSlice<T>, n: uint, compare: func(T, T) -> Ordering) -> bool {
    let data = items.data;
    let length = items.length;
    if n >= length { return false; }

    var lo: uint = 0;
    var hi = length;
    var budget = FloorLog2(length) * 2;
    if budget == 0 {
        budget = 1;
    }

    while hi - lo > InsertionSortThreshold {
        if budget == 0 {
            HeapSortRangeBy<T>(data, lo, hi, compare);
            return true;
        }
        budget--;

        let middle = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
        let pivotIndex = MedianOfThreeBy<T>(data, lo, middle, hi - 1, compare);
        let pivot = data[pivotIndex];

        var lessEnd: uint = 0;
        var greaterStart: uint = 0;
        PartitionThreeWayBy<T>(data, lo, hi, pivot, @lessEnd, @greaterStart, compare);

        if n < lessEnd {
            hi = lessEnd;
        }
        else if n < greaterStart {
            return true;
        }
        else {
            lo = greaterStart;
        }
    }

    InsertionSortRangeBy<T>(data, lo, hi, compare);
    return true;
}

/// Puts the `count` smallest elements of `items` under `compare` in order at its front.
///
/// `items` is borrowed mutably and rewritten; `count` is read by value; `compare` must be a strict weak ordering.
/// Exactly `PartialSort`, with the order supplied rather than taken from the element type.
///
/// Reports false, changing nothing, when `count` is larger than the length.
///
/// O(n log count) comparisons. Equivalent elements may be reordered.
///
/// https://rux-lang.dev/docs/api/algorithms/partial-sort-by
pub func PartialSortBy<T>(items: MutableSlice<T>, count: uint, compare: func(T, T) -> Ordering) -> bool {
    let data = items.data;
    let length = items.length;
    if count > length { return false; }
    if count == 0 { return true; }

    var i = count / 2;
    while i > 0 {
        i--;
        SiftDownBy<T>(data, 0, i, count, compare);
    }

    var j = count;
    while j < length {
        if compare(data[j], data[0]).IsLess() {
            SwapAt<T>(data, 0, j);
            SiftDownBy<T>(data, 0, 0, count, compare);
        }
        j++;
    }

    HeapSortRangeBy<T>(data, 0, count, compare);
    return true;
}

// Internal helper; not part of the package API.
func MedianOfThreeBy<T>(data: *T, first: uint, middle: uint, last: uint, compare: func(T, T) -> Ordering) -> uint {
    if compare(data[first], data[middle]).IsLess() {
        if compare(data[middle], data[last]).IsLess() { return middle; }
        if compare(data[first], data[last]).IsLess() { return last; }
        return first;
    }

    if compare(data[first], data[last]).IsLess() { return first; }
    if compare(data[middle], data[last]).IsLess() { return last; }
    return middle;
}

// Internal helper; not part of the package API.
func PartitionThreeWayBy<T>(data: *var T, low: uint, high: uint, pivot: T, lessEnd: *var uint,
                            greaterStart: *var uint, compare: func(T, T) -> Ordering) {
    var lower = low;
    var index = low;
    var greater = high;

    while index < greater {
        if compare(data[index], pivot).IsLess() {
            SwapAt<T>(data, index, lower);
            lower++;
            index++;
        }
        else {
            if compare(pivot, data[index]).IsLess() {
                greater--;
                SwapAt<T>(data, index, greater);
            }
            else {
                index++;
            }
        }
    }

    *lessEnd = lower;
    *greaterStart = greater;
}

// Internal helper; not part of the package API.
func InsertionSortRangeBy<T>(data: *var T, low: uint, high: uint, compare: func(T, T) -> Ordering) {
    if high - low < 2 { return; }

    var i = low + 1;
    while i < high {
        let key = data[i];
        var j = i;
        while j > low {
            if !compare(key, data[j - 1]).IsLess() { break; }
            data[j] = data[j - 1];
            j--;
        }
        data[j] = key;
        i++;
    }
}

// Internal helper; not part of the package API.
func SiftDownBy<T>(data: *var T, low: uint, start: uint, count: uint, compare: func(T, T) -> Ordering) {
    var root = start;
    loop {
        let child = root * 2 + 1;
        if child >= count { return; }

        var largest = child;
        let right = child + 1;
        if right < count {
            if compare(data[low + child], data[low + right]).IsLess() {
                largest = right;
            }
        }

        if !compare(data[low + root], data[low + largest]).IsLess() { return; }
        SwapAt<T>(data, low + root, low + largest);
        root = largest;
    }
}

// Internal helper; not part of the package API.
func HeapSortRangeBy<T>(data: *var T, low: uint, high: uint, compare: func(T, T) -> Ordering) {
    let count = high - low;
    if count < 2 { return; }

    var i = count / 2;
    while i > 0 {
        i--;
        SiftDownBy<T>(data, low, i, count, compare);
    }

    var end = count;
    while end > 1 {
        end--;
        SwapAt<T>(data, low, low + end);
        SiftDownBy<T>(data, low, 0, end, compare);
    }
}

// Internal helper; not part of the package API.
func IntroSortRangeBy<T>(data: *var T, low: uint, high: uint, depth: uint, compare: func(T, T) -> Ordering) {
    var lo = low;
    var hi = high;
    var budget = depth;

    while hi - lo > InsertionSortThreshold {
        if budget == 0 {
            HeapSortRangeBy<T>(data, lo, hi, compare);
            return;
        }
        budget--;

        let middle = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
        let pivotIndex = MedianOfThreeBy<T>(data, lo, middle, hi - 1, compare);
        let pivot = data[pivotIndex];

        var lessEnd: uint = 0;
        var greaterStart: uint = 0;
        PartitionThreeWayBy<T>(data, lo, hi, pivot, @lessEnd, @greaterStart, compare);

        if lessEnd - lo < hi - greaterStart {
            IntroSortRangeBy<T>(data, lo, lessEnd, budget, compare);
            lo = greaterStart;
        }
        else {
            IntroSortRangeBy<T>(data, greaterStart, hi, budget, compare);
            hi = lessEnd;
        }
    }

    InsertionSortRangeBy<T>(data, lo, hi, compare);
}
